  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 1210 AM CDT Mon Jul 13 2026\n\nThe main focus Tuesday through Thursday will be on the potential for \nheavy rainfall and flooding/flash flooding. On Tuesday an elongated,\nanomalously strong upper ridge will extend from the central Rockies\neast across the Midwest, then east-southeast to the mid- Atlantic\nCoast. To the south of this ridge, an upper low will shift west \nto the lower Mississippi Valley, and there are indications for \nanother low to develop by evening over the Texas Hill Country. The\ncoverage looks uncertain for showers and thunderstorms in our area\nTuesday. The airmass will be very moist (precipitable water values\n1.5 to 2 inches) and uncapped, and will likely have leftover \noutflow boundaries from Monday convection. At this time, leaning \ntoward a scenario with scattered showers and thunderstorms, with \nour and southern counties more favored for higher coverage.\n\nThe aforementioned Texas upper low is forecast to move west-\nnorthwest along or just south of I-10 Tuesday night and Wednesday,\nto near or just north of the Big Bend area by Thursday. This \ntrack and placement would provide more favorable lift with better \nupper support over our area. Should have more organized/widespread\nconvection over our southern and central counties, perhaps with a\nfew rounds of activity. Seeing indications of a weak low-level \njet developing Wednesday night and a little stronger low-level jet\nThursday night. This will help to provide an influx of low-level \nmoisture, and precipitable water values are forecast to be 1.5 to \n1.9 inches. Taking all of these factors into consideration, have \nincreased concerns for flooding/flash flooding in a fairly broad \ntime frame, developing Tuesday night/Wednesday and continuing \nThursday into Thursday night. The main focus looks to be over the \nsouthern and central parts of our area. \n\nWith the above mentioned scenario and increased cloud cover, \ndaytime temperatures Tuesday through Thursday should be limited to\nmostly the 80s for highs. \n\nThe upper low is progged to move slowly west-southwest into far \nnorthern Mexico by Friday night into Saturday. With a slower \nprogression now indicated, the chance of showers and thunderstorms\nwill linger in at least our western counties on Friday. The weekend\nwill trend warmer and drier with gradually building heights aloft.\nMedium range models differ on placement/repositioning of an upper\nlevel ridge, and its proximity to our area. The forecast at this \ntime has highs ranging from the upper 80s/lower 90s in our southern\ncounties, to the mid 90s in the Big Country north of I-20 Saturday,\nand mid to upper 90s Sunday.",
